in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @nl

# @mirror/data — mirror's data-grammar family root.
#
# The recognition (2026-06-07, Alex → Mara): the substrate already
# has `grammar @data/json` declared at the boot altitude
# (boot/std/data/json.mirror, 2026-05-20). What it lacked was the
# family root: the named parent under which yaml, toml, text, and
# the future cbor/msgpack sit as parallel species. This shard lifts
# the JSON declaration from grammar-altitude `@data/json` to mirror-
# altitude `@mirror/data/json` under a family root, matching the
# rhythm of `@code → @mirror/code` (no — `@code` stayed where it
# was; the analogous lift was `@cli → @mirror/lens/cli`) and the
# loss family lift (`@loss → @mirror/loss`). Same primitive at two
# altitudes; the family root names the discipline; species
# parameterize the format.
#
# Load-bearing recognition: **data-grammar IS a lens.** Per the
# glass-IS-lens 23rd-instance recognition (per
# `shards/mirror/loss.mirror`'s family-root recognition; per
# `shards/mirror/lens.mirror`'s six-species transport/measurement
# closure), a data-format grammar IS a typed surface that projects
# bytes through a carrier type and back, preserving the algebra.
# JSON-text is a lens onto a `json` carrier; YAML-text is a lens onto
# a `yaml` carrier; TOML-text onto `toml`; raw text onto `text`. The
# data-grammar species do for content what the lens species do for
# observation: same typed-surface-with-restriction-map shape, one
# altitude lower (the carrier IS the section; parse/emit are the
# restriction maps over the bytes/carrier boundary).
#
# Same `@code/X` precedent extended: `@code/X` (language-grammar
# lens onto source code) and `@mirror/data/X` (data-grammar lens onto
# structured content) are the two halves of grammar-as-lens at the
# mirror altitude. The first half landed at `shards/code.mirror`
# (2026-06-06, the @code family root recognition); this shard lands
# the second half. The four-format opening (json / yaml / toml /
# text) mirrors the four-language opening of @code
# (rust / mirror / gleam / llvm) — sibling families at the same
# altitude, both projecting the same five-op algebra through their
# own typed surfaces.
#
# === Discipline ===
#
# A `@mirror/data` species IS:
#
#   - a typed-surface-with-restriction-map: same five-operation
#     algebra, different carrier (the format's structural type)
#     chosen at the species altitude. Each species declares its
#     carrier type plus parse(text)/emit(carrier)->text — the two
#     restriction maps that move between the format's surface
#     (text) and its substrate (the carrier).
#   - a lens onto bytes: parse(text)->carrier IS the focus
#     projection at the data altitude; emit(carrier)->text IS the
#     settle projection. The five-op block at the family root
#     declares the algebra; each species realises it for one
#     format. Composition is by carrier (you can settle a json
#     into bytes, parse those bytes back, and the resulting json
#     is structurally equal — the standard idempotence law for
#     data-format lenses).
#   - format-as-lens, not parser-as-library: per
#     [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]], the species
#     declares the SHAPE of the lens (carrier + parse + emit); the
#     bodies are `\` (substrate-pull-discharge). Consumers (mcp
#     serve; mirror kintsugi; future @data/cbor) pull the species
#     and get the typed surface; the bodies land when the consumer
#     pulls (per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]]).
#
# === Species under this family ===
#
# Canonical species (lifted from boot, lands this tick):
#
#   @mirror/data/json    JSON. The canonical structured-data
#                        species. Carrier: `null | bool | number
#                        | string | array | object`. Per
#                        boot/std/data/json.mirror's original
#                        declaration; lifted to the family altitude
#                        with `grammar` → `prism` keyword change
#                        per the @code/rust precedent (boot keeps
#                        `grammar @code/rust`; shards/ lifts to
#                        `prism @code/rust`).
#
# Parallel species (stubs land this tick; bodies forward-promised):
#
#   @mirror/data/yaml    YAML 1.2 core schema. Carrier: structurally
#                        analogous to json plus tags. Forward-promised;
#                        bodies land when a consumer pulls
#                        (per docs/specs/pretty-printer-LRM cascade T19).
#   @mirror/data/toml    TOML 1.0. Carrier: similar to json plus
#                        offset_datetime / local_datetime / local_date /
#                        local_time. Forward-promised.
#   @mirror/data/text    Raw text. Carrier: the substrate's native
#                        `text` type. The trivial lens (parse = identity;
#                        emit = identity). Lands as a species so the
#                        family closes over the "unstructured" case at
#                        the same surface as the structured ones —
#                        consumers can dispatch over the family without
#                        a special-case escape hatch.
#
# Future species (no shard yet; named here so the recognition is
# logged):
#
#   @mirror/data/cbor      CBOR (RFC 8949). Binary structured data.
#   @mirror/data/msgpack   MessagePack. Binary structured data.
#   @mirror/data/csv       CSV (RFC 4180). Tabular text.
#   @mirror/data/xml       XML 1.0. Hierarchical text.
#
# === Why one family ===
#
# All species parameterize the same parse/emit shape over a carrier.
# Without the family, every consumer (`mcp serve` invoking json;
# `mirror kintsugi` invoking the pretty-printer cascade; a future
# `@data/cbor` for portal-stream serialization per the
# 2026-06-07 portal-as-eigenvalue-stream insight) lands its format
# at an ad-hoc altitude (`@json`, `@yaml`, etc.) and the cross-cutting
# discipline (carrier-as-section, parse-as-focus, emit-as-settle,
# idempotence-on-roundtrip) has to be re-justified per format. With
# the family, the discipline IS the family.
#
# === The pretty-printer cascade ===
#
# This shard is T16 in the 5-tick cascade closing the pretty-printer
# LRM (per the 2026-06-07 research):
#
#   T16 — @mirror/data root + json lift + yaml/toml/text stubs (this).
#   T17 — @code/mirror.render with Wadler/Bernardy combinators.
#   T18 — eigenvalue projection action on portal stream.
#   T19 — `mirror kintsugi <file>` settled-formatting output path.
#   T20 — portal handoff via SCM_RIGHTS at CLI pipe boundary.
#
# T17 onward consume this family root: `@code/mirror.render` is the
# AST-to-text projection at the mirror grammar altitude (a sibling
# operation to `@mirror/data/json.emit` — code-as-data, rendered
# through the Wadler/Bernardy doc combinators). T19's settled-
# formatting output reads the species roster declared here to know
# what file extensions to dispatch over (.json → @mirror/data/json;
# .yaml → @mirror/data/yaml; .toml → @mirror/data/toml; bare → text).
# T20's portal handoff serializes Beam<T> chunks through one of
# this family's species (likely json initially; cbor when binary
# transport pulls).
#
# === Sibling relationships ===
#
# `@code/X` (shards/code.mirror) is the LANGUAGE-grammar family at
# the mirror altitude. `@mirror/data/X` (this shard) is the DATA-
# grammar family at the mirror altitude. Both name typed lenses
# onto structured byte streams; both declare the five-op block at
# their root; both species-parameterize the carrier type. The
# difference is altitude of the surface: `@code/X` is source-code-
# shaped (parsing is per language grammar; emit is back to source);
# `@mirror/data/X` is data-content-shaped (parsing is per format
# spec; emit is back to wire bytes). The two families partition
# what mirror can read and write at the byte level.
#
# `@mirror/lens` (shards/mirror/lens.mirror) is the OBSERVATION-
# surface family. `@mirror/loss` (shards/mirror/loss.mirror) is
# the MEASUREMENT-carrier family. This shard's siblings are the
# CONTENT-format family. Four mirror-altitude families now: lens
# observes the substrate's running state; loss measures the
# substrate's running residual; data carries the substrate's
# running content through bytes; spec declares the manifold the
# substrate runs over.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
# Each format below names an established serialization grammar:
#
#   json   Crockford (2001); RFC 8259 (2017). The minimal
#          structural-data interchange grammar. JavaScript's
#          object-literal subset, generalized.
#   yaml   Ben-Kiki/Evans/döt Net (2001); YAML 1.2 (2009).
#          Human-readable serialization with indentation
#          structure; superset of JSON.
#   toml   Preston-Werner (2013); TOML 1.0 (2021). Minimal,
#          INI-derived configuration grammar; explicit type
#          discipline.
#   text   Native byte sequences in the substrate's text type;
#          the trivial format (no carrier transformation).
#
# Per [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]: `prism
# @mirror/data { ... }` declares the family IS a prism. The five-
# operation block below is the canonical declaration form (cf.
# shards/mirror/lens.mirror, shards/mirror/loss.mirror,
# shards/mirror/spectral.mirror, shards/mirror/spec.mirror). Each
# species specialises from there.
#
# Spec context: docs/research/2026-06-07-pretty-printer-LRM.md
#               (the 5-tick cascade this family enables),
#               docs/specs/portal-as-eigenvalue-stream.md
#               (the T20 portal handoff that pulls @mirror/data
#               for chunk serialization).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
#                      (the same lift that gave @cli → @mirror/lens/cli
#                      and @loss → @mirror/loss; grammar-altitude
#                      @data → mirror-altitude @mirror/data).
# Substrate recognitions: [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]
#                      (the @data grammar already named json; this
#                      family lifts what was already there into the
#                      mirror altitude with named siblings),
#                      [[feedback-substrate-pull]] (the family root
#                      pulls data from grammar floor to mirror
#                      altitude; no invention, only lift),
#                      [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] (yaml/toml/text
#                      bodies are forward-promised; lands when the
#                      pretty-printer cascade T19 pulls).
# Forward-promise to consumers:
#   - @mirror/data/yaml  bodies land when a YAML-emitting consumer
#                        pulls (likely the kintsugi pretty-printer
#                        cascade T19 over .yaml inputs).
#   - @mirror/data/toml  bodies land when a TOML-emitting consumer
#                        pulls (likely Cargo.toml / pyproject.toml
#                        kintsugi over T19).
#   - @mirror/data/text  bodies land when the dispatch-over-extension
#                        case in T19 pulls the trivial lens.
#   - boot/std/data/json.mirror IS retained as a transitional
#                        placeholder; boot/std/mcp.mirror still
#                        imports `in @data/json`. Migration of
#                        boot consumers to `in @mirror/data/json`
#                        is a separate consumer-pull tick. The
#                        same pattern as @cli (kept in boot) and
#                        @loss (kept in boot) during their lifts.
# Related shards: code.mirror (the sibling language-grammar family),
#                 mirror/lens.mirror (the sibling observation family),
#                 mirror/loss.mirror (the sibling measurement family),
#                 mirror/spec.mirror (the consumer that will pull
#                 @mirror/data/toml for spec.toml emission, future),
#                 boot/std/data/json.mirror (the floor declaration
#                 this family lifts; retained as transitional
#                 placeholder during consumer migration),
#                 boot/std/mcp.mirror (the consumer still importing
#                 `in @data/json`; migration deferred to a separate
#                 tick).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/mirror/data.mirror`
# declares `@mirror/data` as the family root; species live at
# `shards/mirror/data/<name>.mirror`.

prism @mirror/data {
  focus data
  project data
  split data
  shift data
  settle data
}

out @mirror/data
